Assistant: The chilling stage represents a crucial control point for reducing Campylobacter contamination on poultry. Immersion chilling, when properly managed with fresh, chlorinated water maintained at near-freezing temperatures, creates an environment hostile to bacterial survival while the constant water flow helps wash away surface contamination. The key is ensuring the water remains clean through continuous replenishment and appropriate antimicrobial levels. Air chilling offers a different approach to safety—by avoiding water altogether, it eliminates any risk of moisture-mediated cross-contamination while the cold, dry environment naturally suppresses bacterial growth. Each method, when executed with meticulous attention to temperature control and sanitation protocols, can deliver meaningful reductions in pathogen levels. The key thing is maintaining the cold chain integrity and ensuring that whichever system is used operates at peak efficiency. For home cooks, this translates to selecting poultry from suppliers who demonstrate commitment to proper post-processing handling, regardless of their chilling method, and maintaining that careful temperature control through storage and preparation.

