User: How do different egg washing and sanitizing practices affect shell permeability and internal contamination?
Assistant: The natural bloom on fresh eggs serves as nature's own protective barrier, sealing thousands of microscopic pores and keeping the interior pristine. When eggs are washed—especially in commercial operations using chlorinated water heated to 115-120°F—this delicate coating dissolves away, leaving the shell vulnerable. I've observed that farm-fresh, unwashed eggs maintain their quality for weeks at cool room temperature, while washed eggs require immediate refrigeration to prevent moisture loss and bacterial migration through those newly exposed pores. The difference is remarkable: properly handled unwashed eggs show almost no internal contamination after three weeks, while washed eggs can develop issues even under refrigeration. For those who keep backyard flocks, I recommend leaving clean eggs unwashed, storing them pointed-end down, and only washing immediately before use with water at least 20°F warmer than the egg to prevent contaminants from being drawn inside. If you must wash soiled eggs, use plain warm water, dry them thoroughly, and refrigerate immediately. This mindful approach honors the egg's natural defenses while ensuring food safety.

